// Expected: c0 at one session and at two (GitHub issue #43). It is still c0
// at three sessions and c1 at four.
//
// secret is split four ways and every share is needed to recompose it. The
// three shares Alice leaks are wrapped under wrap, and Bob decrypts only what
// arrives as eone, leaking the result. Each Bob run can therefore unwrap one
// share: the attacker replaces eone with a different leaked wrapping in each
// run, and the fourth share is Bob's honest one. With fewer than four runs one
// share is always missing, so the results at one, two and three sessions agree
// and still say nothing about four. This is why Verifpal has no saturation mode
// that stops raising the count once adjacent counts agree.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private secret, wrap
	sone, stwo, sthree, sfour = THRESHOLD_SPLIT[4](secret)
	eone = ENC(wrap, sone)
	etwo = ENC(wrap, stwo)
	ethree = ENC(wrap, sthree)
	efour = ENC(wrap, sfour)
	leaks etwo, ethree, efour
]

Alice -> Bob: eone

principal Bob[
	knows private wrap
	d = DEC(wrap, eone)
	leaks d
]

queries[
	confidentiality? secret
]
